A foreign key guarantees the referenced row exists. Actions: `NO ACTION`/`RESTRICT`, `CASCADE`, `SET NULL`, `SET DEFAULT`. PostgreSQL does not index the referencing column; add an index on every foreign key you join or delete by.

```sql
CREATE TABLE orders (
  id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES customers(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT
);
CREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id);
```

A junction table with foreign keys to both sides and a composite primary key, plus attributes of the relationship. One-to-one: the child's primary key is also a foreign key to the parent. Index the second column of the composite key for lookups from the other side.

```sql
CREATE TABLE product_tags (
  product_id bigint REFERENCES products(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  tag_id     bigint REFERENCES tags(id)     ON DELETE CASCADE,
  PRIMARY KEY (product_id, tag_id)
);
CREATE INDEX ON product_tags (tag_id, product_id);
```

Use columns for data you filter, join, constrain or report on. Use `jsonb` for sparse, variable or externally shaped attributes. Costs: no type or FK enforcement, weak planner statistics, rewriting the whole value on update. Index with GIN or an expression index; promote hot keys to columns.

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_events_payload ON events USING gin (payload jsonb_path_ops);
SELECT * FROM events WHERE payload @> '{"type":"signup"}';
```

A sargable predicate lets the engine use an index. Functions on the column hide it. Rewrite to a range on the raw column, or create an expression index matching the expression exactly.

```sql
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at >= '2026-10-01' AND created_at < '2026-10-02';
CREATE INDEX idx_customers_lower_email ON customers (lower(email));
```

A B-tree cannot help with a leading wildcard. Use `pg_trgm` with GIN/GiST, full-text search for words, `text_pattern_ops` for prefix search, or a search engine for heavy needs.

```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;
CREATE INDEX idx_customers_name_trgm ON customers USING gin (name gin_trgm_ops);
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE name ILIKE '%silva%';
```

The planner estimates rows from `pg_stats`. Stale or insufficient statistics produce bad plans. `ANALYZE` refreshes them; raise the statistics target for skewed columns; `CREATE STATISTICS` for correlated columns.

```sql
ANALYZE orders;
CREATE STATISTICS st_city_zip (dependencies) ON city, zip FROM addresses;
```

A prepared statement is parsed once and run with parameters (and prevents SQL injection). PostgreSQL plans the first executions with real values (custom plans) and may switch to a generic plan; skewed data can make it bad. Behind a transaction-mode pooler, protocol-level prepared statements need pooler support.

```sql
PREPARE by_cust (bigint) AS SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1;
EXECUTE by_cust(7);
```

A waiting `ALTER` blocks everything behind it, so set `lock_timeout` and retry. `ADD COLUMN` with a constant default is instant; build indexes `CONCURRENTLY`; add FKs `NOT VALID` then validate. Expand and contract: add, dual-write, backfill, switch reads, remove old.

```sql
SET lock_timeout = '3s';
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN currency text;
ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT chk_cur CHECK (currency IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;
ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT chk_cur; -- weaker lock
```

CDC streams committed changes out of the database (logical decoding, Debezium). The outbox writes the business change and an event row in one transaction; a relay publishes it, avoiding the dual-write problem. Consumers must be idempotent because delivery is at least once.

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET status = 'paid' WHERE id = 42;
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_id, type, payload) VALUES (42, 'OrderPaid', '{"id":42}');
COMMIT;
```

A view stores a query and runs it each time. A materialized view stores the result and is refreshed on demand. `REFRESH ... CONCURRENTLY` avoids blocking readers but needs a unique index.

```sql
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW daily_sales AS
  SELECT date_trunc('day', created_at) AS day, sum(total_cents) AS cents FROM orders GROUP BY 1;
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON daily_sales (day);
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY daily_sales;
```

`->` returns jsonb, `->>` text, `#>` a path, `@>` containment, `?` key exists, plus SQL/JSON path. Index with GIN (`jsonb_path_ops` for `@>`) or an expression index on one key.

```sql
SELECT payload->>'email' FROM events WHERE payload @> '{"type":"signup"}';
CREATE INDEX idx_events_type ON events ((payload->>'type'));
```

Documents become `tsvector` (stemmed lexemes), queries `tsquery`; `@@` matches; `ts_rank` scores. For typo tolerance and facets, a search engine.

```sql
ALTER TABLE articles ADD COLUMN tsv tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (to_tsvector('english', coalesce(title,'') || ' ' || coalesce(body,''))) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_articles_tsv ON articles USING gin (tsv);
SELECT title FROM articles WHERE tsv @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', 'postgres index');
```

Policies on a table decide row visibility, enforced in the database. Common for multitenancy with a session variable per request. Owners and superusers bypass unless `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`; test the overhead.

```sql
ALTER TABLE campaigns ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON campaigns
  USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::bigint);
```

Range types represent intervals with operators like `&&` (overlap). An exclusion constraint forbids overlapping ranges for the same resource, race-free.

```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;
CREATE TABLE bookings (
  room_id int, period tstzrange,
  EXCLUDE USING gist (room_id WITH =, period WITH &&)
);
```

`RETURNING` gives back affected rows in one round trip. A data-modifying CTE chains writes. `generate_series` produces numbers or dates for calendars, gap-filling and test data.

```sql
WITH moved AS (
  DELETE FROM orders WHERE created_at < now() - interval '2 years' RETURNING *
)
INSERT INTO orders_archive SELECT * FROM moved;

SELECT d::date FROM generate_series('2026-10-01'::date, '2026-10-07', '1 day') d;
```

Find duplicates with `GROUP BY ... HAVING count(*) > 1`; to delete, rank within each group and delete rank > 1. Add a unique constraint afterwards.

```sql
DELETE FROM customers c
USING (
  SELECT id, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn FROM customers
) d
WHERE c.id = d.id AND d.rn > 1;

ALTER TABLE customers ADD CONSTRAINT uq_customers_email UNIQUE (email);
```

State the invariants first, then enforce them with keys and constraints, and make writes idempotent and transactional.

```sql
CREATE TABLE products (
  id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  sku text NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  stock int NOT NULL CHECK (stock >= 0)
);
CREATE TABLE payments (
  id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  order_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES orders(id),
  idempotency_key text NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  amount_cents bigint NOT NULL CHECK (amount_cents > 0)
);
-- reserve stock atomically: fails the CHECK instead of overselling
UPDATE products SET stock = stock - $2 WHERE id = $1;
```
